Intro — ~12 sec

[Two images]

“Can you tell which one is AI-generated?

I bet you couldn’t.

That’s why, in the last 72 hours, we built TrueSight — a multi-tiered AI detection and media provenance system.”

Pipeline — ~15 sec

[3-tier diagram]

“Instead of running expensive models on every image, TrueSight progressively routes media through three tiers: cryptographic provenance, deterministic forensics with semantic analysis, and finally, ConvNeXt-based AI detection.”

Tier 1 — ~15 sec

[C2PA diagram]

“Tier One checks C2PA credentials to cryptographically verify an image’s origin and editing history.

If provenance exists, we get fast, deterministic evidence without expensive inference.”

Tier 2 — ~22 sec

[ELA / High-pass / ORB → Mask → VLM]

“If metadata is missing, Tier Two examines the pixels themselves.

ELA, high-pass filtering, and ORB feature matching detect compression, noise, and duplication anomalies, producing a spatial tamper mask.

A Vision Language Model then examines those regions for semantic inconsistencies.

The math tells us where. The VLM tells us why.”

Tier 3 + Grad-CAM — ~20 sec

[ConvNeXt → Grad-CAM]

“For sophisticated AI images that bypass traditional forensics, Tier Three uses a custom-tuned ConvNeXt model to detect subtle learned patterns associated with synthetic media.

And with Grad-CAM, we highlight the regions that actually drove its decision.”

Fusion — ~10 sec

[Late Fusion]

“Finally, late fusion combines provenance, forensic evidence, semantic analysis, and ConvNeXt into one calibrated decision.

We don’t trust one detector. We combine independent evidence.”

